WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.150
Levels 4 and 5 broadly represent enterprise planning, business systems, corporate users,

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:06.620
and external connectivity.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.790
Organizations describe these levels differently,

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:13.140
but common examples include enterprise resource planning, corporate identity, finance,

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:17.600
email, user workstations, data platforms, internet services, and cloud connections.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.000
These systems support the business around production.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.810
They create schedules, manage materials and orders, provide workforce identities,

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:25.400
and consume operational data.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.250
Because they serve many users and connect to external services,

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:32.315
they are exposed to common enterprise threats such as phishing, credential compromise,

00:00:32.315 --> 00:00:35.655
ransomware, vulnerable applications, and third-party access.

00:00:35.655 --> 00:00:38.914
Enterprise compromise can create OT risk through shared dependencies.

00:00:38.914 --> 00:00:42.300
A corporate identity outage may affect authorized remote support.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.000
Ransomware may disrupt production scheduling or files needed by operations.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.241
A compromised user account may reach a poorly controlled jump host.

00:00:49.241 --> 00:00:53.600
A cloud integration may create a path that bypasses the intended industrial DMZ.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.825
The Purdue view helps teams resist direct connections that are convenient but unsafe.

00:00:57.825 --> 00:01:01.590
Enterprise applications should exchange required data through controlled services.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:04.355
Corporate users should not administer controllers directly.

00:01:04.355 --> 00:01:08.270
Identity and remote-access designs should preserve strong governance without creating a

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:11.415
single unexamined dependency for critical operations.

00:01:11.415 --> 00:01:15.215
Security teams should map cross-level business flows, including scheduling, reporting,

00:01:15.215 --> 00:01:17.140
support, updates, and authentication.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.770
For each flow, identify what happens if it is unavailable, altered, or misused.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:25.295
Enterprise and OT teams share responsibility for these boundaries.

00:01:25.295 --> 00:01:29.425
Protecting Level 4 and Level 5 is part of OT risk reduction because the enterprise

00:01:29.425 --> 00:01:31.520
environment often supplies the pathways,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.900
identities, and services that connect to operations.
